  "full_response": "Processes and threads are fundamental concepts in operating systems that deal with the execution of programs. Here’s a detailed comparison to explain the differences between them:\n\n### Processes\n\n1. **Definition**: \n   - A process is an instance of a program that is being executed. It is an active entity, containing the program code and its current activity.\n\n2. **Context Switching**: \n   - Switching between processes is known as a context switch. It is generally more expensive in terms of time and resources than switching threads because it involves loading new memory maps among other tasks.\n\n3. **Memory Space**:\n   - Each process has its own separate memory space. This means that a process cannot directly access another process's memory, which helps in protection and security.\n\n4. **System Resource Requirements**:\n   - Since processes are independent, each one needs its own system resources like memory, file handles, etc.\n\n5. **Execution**:\n   - Each process in a system has its own set of resources and state. For example, multiple processes can be running at the same time but are independent of one another.\n\n6. **Communication**:\n   - Communication between processes is more complex and can involve inter-process communication (IPC) mechanisms such as pipes, message queues, shared memory, or sockets.\n\n7. **Lifecycle**:\n   - A process goes through several stages of its lifecycle including creation, running, waiting, ready, and termination.\n\n### Threads\n\n1. **Definition**: \n   - A thread, often called a lightweight process, is a component of a process. It is the smallest unit of processing that can be scheduled by an operating system.\n\n2. **Context Switching**:\n   - Switching between threads of the same process is generally much faster than switching between processes because threads within the same process share the same memory space and resources.\n\n3. **Memory Space**:\n   - Threads within the same process share the process’s memory space — including code segments, data segments, and the OS resources like open files and signals. This makes communication between threads easier and faster than between processes.\n\n4. **System Resource Requirements**: \n   - Because threads within the same process share resources, less resource allocation is needed for each thread as opposed to separate processes.\n\n5. **Execution**:\n   - Multiple threads can execute concurrently within the same process. If a process has multiple threads, they may be executing with overlapping timelines.\n\n6. **Communication**:\n   - Since threads share the address space, they can communicate with each other by simply accessing",
